Stage change rows with an explicit schema, and stop out-stricting the gateway

Two faults stood between a generated notebook and a working sync. Both
were found by running the notebook's own code in a Spark session, because
Fabric reports every cell failure as the same sentence: "System cancelled
the Spark session due to statement execution failures".

The passthrough rejected the notebook's own page size. The gateway caps a
page at 1000 frames and advertises the rest through x-asmdb-has-more, so
it accepts a larger limit and returns fewer; the notebook relies on that
and asks for 5000. The new route validated the limit at 1000 and returned
400, which made it stricter than the gateway it fronts. It now clamps,
which is what the gateway itself does.

Staging then failed on schema inference. Rows are built with a fixed set
of columns, several of which are legitimately null in every row: a table
that never sets value or tag, and _synced_at always, because that column
is filled in after staging. Spark cannot infer a type for a column that
is null throughout and raises CANNOT_DETERMINE_TYPE, failing the sync.

Both write paths now build the frame from a declared schema. Decoder
columns, whose names depend on the decoder, take their type from the
first non-null value and default to string. The ISO timestamps are parsed
back into datetimes so they land in a timestamp column rather than
silently becoming strings and breaking the MERGE against an existing
table.

Verified end to end against the live engine: five rows through gateway,
backend and Spark into Delta, with the correct column types and the
watermark property written after the data.

